  "title": "Time: chron, temp, ann and nov",
  "short": "chron (Greek) and temp (Latin) mean time, ann and enn mean year, and nov means new.",
  "trouble": "chronic means long-lasting, not severe; perennial hides enn (per + annus, \"through the years\").",
  "prereqs": [
    "roots.latin-greek"
  ],
  "xp": 1,
  "viz": [
    "vo-roots",
    "vo-word"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "practice": null,
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "Time has a Greek root and a Latin one. Greek <b>chron</b> gives us <i>chronometer</i>, an exact clock. Latin <b>temp</b> (also <b>tempor</b>) gives us <i>temporary</i> (lasting a short time) and <i>tempo</i> (the speed of music). Two more roots measure time: <b>ann</b> (also <b>enn</b>) means “year,” as in <i>annual</i> and <i>anniversary</i>, and <b>nov</b> means “new,” as in <i>renovate</i> (make new again)."
      },
      {
